#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179493 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8033e21-86ab-5dc6-8060-a0b5c134bf9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56f7f36b-4e9c-5a86-bd20-ca01d9fce41f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9412" :question/problem "Consider the expression $f(x,y) = (3-x)(y+1)^2.$  What are the missing entries in the sign table of $f(x,y)$ shown below?\n\n| $y > -1$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <               -1$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<   3$ | $x> 3$ |" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x = 3$ and $y = -1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/df8519853487a7da738b4b3c477b141e00287c67dcb9eb804cbd385c6f43b611.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/4666739c474f85cbf8de7e7ca183206ab7b0bd804816a5504f9c1d97b03956b4.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(3-x)$ | $(y+1)^2$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(2,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,0)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(2,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-2)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/e789ce41c696b456e0c9d79ba8ce60aba030e454ab614902ea13c8d9ccd02d8c.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y > -1$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <               -1$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n|  | $x<  3$ | $x> 3$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179494 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "222d8f84-be89-5bcf-94f3-f309635bbcbf" :question/math-academy-id "e-9414" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y) = (x-2)^2(y+1).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y) > 0?$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=2$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/8e635d2ba2e7ca5721ff8b1dec2055b4ac0c9bcc2eca648848c27190271e92b6.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/1ce934096086de33423910710a010400922ea86c66bbfe0b1bfb0d92291ab59e.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-2)^2$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(1,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(3,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(1,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(3,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y) > 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/70a2ca01e47e91f9d818599886f79b0f00edad0cfd12b080995dfbe6cdf68235.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8033e21-86ab-5dc6-8060-a0b5c134bf9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f3a246c-800f-5999-9b93-b9ed1cd1d295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296022} {:answer/id #uuid "dd461320-244c-5daa-985d-40605a216206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296023} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7c0266-869b-5f46-9251-f7d45ad77979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296024} {:answer/id #uuid "db6d40c5-e2c0-5946-99d2-7d8f1a508394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296025} {:answer/id #uuid "f5319bce-f88c-581b-a6dd-645e7a59d343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd461320-244c-5daa-985d-40605a216206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296023} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d0a0b78-80b6-526e-91f3-053687642870" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffd06503-0d35-5908-aec5-906d9832f7e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-120971" :question/problem "Consider the expression $f(x,y)=(x-2)(y+1)^{2}.$  What are the missing entries in the sign table of $f(x,y),$ shown below?\n\n| $y>-1$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=2$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/a66a57932dbc935b5ed6f74b4f0faa0047c4b7ef58ded46be088dad30fa5eee4.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/52efe97fcffa3da05151b3ffef5305e0c3ec0d567d3f62b964a30740c08a3981.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-2)$ | $(y+1)^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-2,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-2,-3)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/aa100568c18b31507c775e77ed3201a5aed6f708b541e48046617a43fe9a8304.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0