r regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/599d3a551a20e68989e5de517711ae4c7c73255fb4a9d716797cf90dbb32a4ea.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/43bb5eac3414291bbd844a3608835a6f7be3e5e0dfcc2ae89426581eccdc87b3.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $x$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,0)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-2)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/18757d46e86eb0d771841e7f5fe13be9ad464ccaf66b7190e7a2a7f02428a88e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db8b98c3-4856-53e6-9697-149247cc060d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121092-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177257} {:answer/id #uuid "e260a6fb-fdfc-5be5-b6b6-5c714c5e151b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121092-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177258} {:answer/id #uuid "022ee7a9-4f27-55e0-9831-4674bce7c31f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121092-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177259} {:answer/id #uuid "1b944579-e38c-532a-bdc7-45a937ed1f4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121092-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177260} {:answer/id #uuid "51478ed4-48e2-5e1f-afe2-45a2128b3dbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121092-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "022ee7a9-4f27-55e0-9831-4674bce7c31f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3684/Source/Images/q-121092-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177259} :answer-field/id #uuid "6037a26f-e055-55ef-8673-5d538b998a3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77c2dc9d-e99c-5a65-8e17-97fc8f7373ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-121092" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}(y+1).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)<0?$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/c4aecfe6b9374576ae002e439ff756d41ba29eb87d2f99cf3569dd3332e0128b.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/eedbe9fe606042eb34d0067132e5bfccfea6e6528e3eaf7091f0c237a5fe6ca3.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | ${x}^{2}$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-1,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)<0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/f6c800752188e6dd292d47f7153462077288540dd232f76a03472b2bab3d20c0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296027 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b4d2334-db89-594c-9a9a-53f8136cf3ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/1bd9768a14f9cd4a785dab34e16aa3cb580784b91abaaa57728119910e578d80.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296029} {:answer/id #uuid "75dbb13b-7173-53e8-9bf2-353598cd1a2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/d3783b95073375680613a63e6964020256128ce8452ddad8fb13dddfc3a1a567.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296030} {:answer/id #uuid "9f3eecb3-fe56-50c1-ab53-06117d38dc74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/6d3adc17b4cadfbec159597d585375769300959fcb0cb176b0ccbf469bc98f45.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296031} {:answer/id #uuid "c31a6458-29cf-58f5-852a-bbedd829531d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/be5f2602a9f1233bb4e5227a42ff29bb8f2c88e13603384d32d64b0355271594.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296032} {:answer/id #uuid "e0267afc-8e85-5938-9eda-d8537c702617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/9c7bd1818e2315a950dc2de3e84d35f449eb9627803e21e29ceb618e98cff885.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c31a6458-29cf-58f5-852a-bbedd829531d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/be5f2602a9f1233bb4e5227a42ff29bb8f2c88e13603384d32d64b0355271594.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296032} :answer-field/id #uuid "bec33654-ca92-5956-a798-6700ffd5376a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "079fdbd8-192c-5486-bfcb-d0943d20768e" :question/math-academy-id "q-121267" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=(x+2)(y-1).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)\\le 0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=-2$ and $y=1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/ce8f784a51d18fd9ea97672b6642b643947845e87b0a4d4c7dd291fcb18f50ed.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/c53a92d71b292288e566db12011c13569ebee65e879598e10b6a14e3acd4f189.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x+2)$ | $(y-1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-3,2)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(-1,2)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-3,0)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(-1,0)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\le 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/4d65d4c9829b7161152722b7cf6e8605c1cace8078e53430f3480d5d16d74ec4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8033e21-86ab-5dc6-8060-a0b5c134bf9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions Where a Function Is Positive or Negative Using a Sign Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b7a642b-9289-5658-8cb2-6430ef4f3657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296036} {:answer/id #uuid "ad81dbd0-4ae6-5940-9f26-4d67ac5e35d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $-$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296037} {:answer/id #uuid "a57c31df-88af-5f8e-9465-613dd10689f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296038} {:answer/id #uuid "249e28ea-bf16-5fcd-980a-2359b725aba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296039} {:answer/id #uuid "11dfa3d2-deed-5a7a-a685-274eb61e61b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "249e28ea-bf16-5fcd-980a-2359b725aba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "| $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $-$ |" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296039} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce2b80e4-b7ae-551f-874b-eced75c2b7c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4617a893-9b55-536f-a921-57c21289c3fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-121274" :question/problem "Consider the expression $f(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}(y+1).$  What are the missing entries in the sign table of $f(x,y),$ shown below?\n\n| $y>-1$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=1$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/23195f0e8657cfbd219f540d6f4403c6bffe1056849e4da43a11b61f4afba76a.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/fad89577df15122a8f80996cbf4f1b840084f51cd93d97a776f5562d59a4cc99.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-1)^{2}$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-2,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-2,-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-2)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/ba5f76185cc6d2e25204c10e8e25b0bf28b3686cb1d494f87af8f03c4b2f2b66.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee89116a-52c8-5866-8cc0-3ee8b75972f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/9505947a6ab1ebfca0aedaeec8cde50e8859dfa7bc2b349833c9d26bd849748b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296043} {:answer/id #uuid "832679bb-ada0-563a-9bf9-7ddd56714444" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/8eac178458e8d2b832f7b96fa5d54fedf94be2a9574666aae48c88fe254a8629.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296044} {:answer/id #uuid "7696613a-467d-5f2c-8480-1ac1b4ada008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/870d9de880f857a1fe66851ae115575955efb12b4cd651279d7494b4cb99d022.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296045} {:answer/id #uuid "40b1af0e-ecf1-5c74-8566-50ed7b5a7d45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/ea79645ad6bf13be7399d8a46d18c7b99e1f2c40d3a6c500ee3710e79fdfddb3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296046} {:answer/id #uuid "9b1b1b96-8832-5ec9-8217-2713b58b546e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/85074c62b73199b0dd1ad8427339c58dae3e1d4ae1df014d94415aea57f24410.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b1b1b96-8832-5ec9-8217-2713b58b546e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/85074c62b73199b0dd1ad8427339c58dae3e1d4ae1df014d94415aea57f24410.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296047} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6a0f068-819e-5f45-991d-4f0f1346b740" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6bb23e0c-4a26-596c-aca7-3529c2ce3d72" :question/math-academy-id "q-121275" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x,y)=(x+2)(2-y).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y)<0?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=-2$ and $y=2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/38f5235039b0a5800651759e8da98166451ab72d25e7eaa9772c10b68c3618ff.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/a68d4ca3939349cef83c3c84117df44ddacf49d587b1bc8a871918f62d9b6516.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x+2)$ | $(2-y)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(-3,3)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(-1,3)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-3,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(-1,1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)<0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/1055b7667e7b1c6d27ad7a9d28ec74ae7ed58dac262ae83b5c4028dcee3f9312.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 175921862