| $x>0$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=0$ and $y=1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/ef935d673ddc4fa3b66305717f495914c194be2abff63611f8f6be6763e469ce.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/6f2f29dbb53e8c20d963cc48c89af73b1c806985bc77842cbe28b882305ede32.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-x$ | $(y-1)^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(-1,2)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(1,2)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(-1,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(1,0)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/71aceb11f3056308300e3e5c9587d76a281379964caab83f640a6924ffd94298.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>1$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ef881ff-71d4-5268-9cac-32c478dc02a5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regions in the Plane Where a Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51cdf062-77d3-53fc-b3a0-4ccd9f0d2e1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/330f6080a3640f193e3d1650405030c049f059aaa9e3c6f280d2355382ed2e69.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296057} {:answer/id #uuid "109d3ab8-3738-54b4-aee0-77f5d905df8f" :answer/type {:db/ident 